[Music]  [Music]  hey everyone its Peter Brown standing in  for freelance reviewer Heidi camps for  her review of Fire Emblem echos shadows  of Valencia Fire Emblem echoes a remake  of a very early game in this series is a  departure of sorts from what most  veteran players expect from a Fire  Emblem game on 3ds rather than  emphasizing character relationships and  story dialogue echoes puts his focus on  long-term and short-term strategy and  strength building the end result is a  fresh take on the Fire Emblem strategy  formula and one that ranks among the  best in the 3ds library echoes follows  the dual leads of on and Celica  a pair of youths that bear a strange  crest upon their hands they bond  together as children in a tiny farming  village only to be torn apart by a  sudden dramatic event many years after  the fact you're in control of both  characters in their respective armies in  search of a reunion amongst a conflict  ridden yarn spun of large scale Wars  hidden pasts and shocking truths while  the story is classic Fire Emblem fare  the emphasis here is centered firmly on  the saga of Amon Celica with only a few  brief interludes that shift focus to  other army members the characters you  welcomed into your ranks and interact  with are charming and likable Bunch and  almost all in-game character text is  voiced as well which adds appreciable  personality  dicks you'll interest pigs huh who the  hell are you players more accustomed to  recent Fire Emblem games like Awakening  and fates may feel a bit disappointed in  the lack of side character interactions  you don't pair off characters and echoes  as you would in those games while  character to character support  conversations do still exist they're  much shorter and happen strictly during  combat while this may be disappointing  to some overall it helps cement the  story focus on the two leads in the  various warring factions of Fire Emblem  echos world while most of echos takes  place on grid-based turn driven  battlefields you'll also spend a lot of  time navigating an overworld map with  two armies one led by aam and the other  by Celica each with a different group of  soldiers under their lead you'll have to  manage resources weaponry and stat  building across two seperate teams  explorable towns castles shrines forts  and dungeons are unique to echoes and  while interactions with most areas are  somewhat limited basically restricted to  examining environments with a cursor  dungeons offer a far more interesting  twist for the seer  dungeons are explored on foot from a  third-person perspective you scout for  secret passages and smash pots and  crates for loot all while avoiding or  seeking out battles against roaming  enemies touching a foe takes you to a  traditional Fire Emblem battle but once  you fill your opponents it's right back  to exploring these areas serve as a  great addition that offer variety beyond  simply stringing together a series of  battles while still keeping the  narrative focus on the core story  I'll face down thieves or rogues any day  but terrace terrors are different hello  Bowie how can you be so scared of spooks  and spirits at your age shove off would  you a man can't help what gives him  fright echoes has some crucial  differences from other Fire Emblem games  that had interesting layers to army  management in combat as well  characters can only carry one item at a  time forcing you to carefully consider  if a special weapon a restorative item  or armaments like a shield and ring  would be ideal to carry magic has  learned through levelling up rather than  buying tomes and uses character hit  points to cast making high-powered  spells a potentially risky proposition  combat skills are learned by keeping  specific items equipped in battle for  long periods of time and are tied to  individual pieces of gear meaning you  can't just learn a skill and then react  whip your weapon as usual stamina wears  down as characters fight in take damage  degrading their stats and combat  capabilities unless they replenish it  with food medicine or offerings to the  goddess Milla finally the  rock-paper-scissors style weapon  triangle of modern Fire Emblem games is  gone entirely swords can now clash with  spheres on equal footing these are all  serious deviations from other Fire  Emblem games and they might take a bit  of time to get used to but they result  in a Fire Emblem game that has a very  distinct feel you'll need to really stop  and think about weapon distribution and  upgrades consider how to effectively use  certain classes when to take time with  optional fights to build character  levels and so on Echo's battles are  generally difficult too which makes  conquering some of the game's toughest  battles relatively unscathed feel like a  real accomplishment while the game's  difficulty level makes formulating a  sound strategy highly rewarding there  are times when grinding for experience  becomes a necessary evil yet it still  manages to feel worth it when you manage  to pull off a victory against unlikely  odds pride inhalation come in equal  measure  [Music]  very good right helping you to secure  those feelings is a brilliant new  addition to gameplay called Mulas turn  wheel which effectively acts as a rewind  button during battle missed several  attacks in a row you can opt to spin  back time a few turns and take a totally  new approach instead you can rewind time  as much or as little as you like  provided you still have enough cogs and  reserve for that battle this wonderful  system allows you to take back critical  combat mistakes without having to reset  a long and arduous battle and is a  tremendous boon in both casual and  classic modes with perma death once you  run out of cogs though you'll have to  restart the level and take back mistakes  adding yet another nice layer of  strategy to this mechanic is it really  worth a cog to re-roll for a critical  hit or should you save it for when you  plan your final assault on the tough as  nails enemy commander only you can make  the call Fire Emblem echoes is a  fantastic remake departure from modern  Fire Emblem staples what it lacks in  interpersonal character relationships  and user control dating it makes up for  immediate challenging strategy gameplay  fun exploration sequences and a tighter  overall narrative it may not be the sort  of game that fans of recent Fire Emblem  games are used to but it thrives for  what it is a challenging tactics game  that puts combat front and center  [Music]  you expected

